/** OC siman 489 batch B — slug:seif:marker translations */
export const SMALL = {
  "beer-hagolah:1:א": `(א) Baraita, Menachot 15`,
  "beer-hagolah:1:ב": `(ב) Plain from Mishnah Megillah 21`,
  "beer-hagolah:1:ג": `(ג) Baraita, Menachot 15`,
  "beer-hagolah:1:ה": `(ה) Statement of Abaye there 15; Rif and Rosh wrote at the end of Pesachim`,
  "beer-hagolah:1:ו": `(ו) Rosh in responsum, and Tur likewise`,
  "beer-hagolah:4:_": `There: Rambam's wording that if one counted without blessing he fulfilled`,
  "beer-hagolah:6:_": `Beit Yosef per what Ran wrote: most commentators agree counting the omer nowadays is d'rabbanan, unlike Tur in the name of Abba ha-Ezri`,
  "beer-hagolah:8:א": `In the name of Halakhot Gedolot, and Tosafot likewise in his name; Menachot 66 as well`,
  "beur-hagra:1:ב": `(ב) How etc. — to exclude those who say one counts the week only at the end of the week; see Tur and Beit Yosef; and to exclude those who say in the second week one need not count the days of the week that passed to say eight days, but only one week and one day alone; see there and there`,
  "beur-hagra:10:א": `(י) Forbidden. Like the anonymous mishnah at the end of Orlah; and although it argues with the anonymous mishnah in chapter 1 of Kiddushin — they said there who is the tanna etc., therefore it is individual and not anonymous; and R' Akiva holds like R' Eliezer; and in Menachot 68b the rabbis of R' Eliezer and Ravina hold it is d'oraisa in chutz la-aretz; see Yoreh De'ah siman 293 seif 3; see what is written with clear proofs that the primary ruling follows R' Eliezer`,
  "beur-hagra:2:א": `(ב) If they erred etc. — as written in Berachot 27b: the community is different etc., unlike here where the community is not required`,
  "beur-hagra:2:ב": `And the punctilious etc. — as written at the beginning of Berachot and at the end of chapter 2 of Megillah; and we rule until stars emerge etc.; and to exclude the view of Tosafot in Menachot 66a s.v. zachar: that which is written since it is a rabbinic uncertainty, nevertheless it is not proper to place oneself in rabbinic uncertainty; and that which is written because of temimot — in Temple times per its law at night, and one should not be stringent in our time more. Ran`,
  "beur-hagra:3:א": `(ג) One who prays etc. — meaning in twilight he fulfills b'dieved since it is rabbinic uncertainty as above, and as written in seif 4; and he holds blessing prevents for this matter that he return and bless, and as written in siman 60 seif 2 regarding Shema, and as written in Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Berachot — examine there. But Rada himself retracted and wrote: but Rambam wrote if he counted without blessing he fulfilled, and so is primary; therefore etc.; and it is possible Shulchan Aruch holds like his first view that from Rambam there is no proof, for there it deals with fulfilling counting, and therefore the Rav wrote in the gloss if his intent etc. only regarding blessing; but in seif 4 he wrote Rada's words in his retraction, and from this there is no proof, as Rashba wrote blessings were not enacted on Shema — as written above; and see in Rashba siman 235 who wondered greatly on this and wrote there that after he wrote thus in the name of Rashi he wrote it is not Rashi signed on it`,
  "beur-hagra:3:ב": `That he not fulfill. Meaning: even for those who say mitzvot do not require intent, as Tosafot wrote Sukkah 39a s.v. over`,
  "beur-hagra:4:ב": `And when the time arrives. As written in chapter 1 of Berachot that a person should not say etc., and in chapter 1 of Shabbat one should not sit etc.; and this is per Tosafot Berachot 4b s.v. ve-korei etc., and Tur siman 235; but per Beit Yosef and as Shulchan Aruch wrote there half an hour before as with Minchah — so too here`,
  "beur-hagra:4:ג": `And even etc. — there they interrupt for Shema; and Rambam and Ravyah hold counting nowadays is d'oraisa; and although Ameimar holds it is d'rabbanan, the rabbis of R' Eliezer and Rava who hold like Abaye — in any case hold it is d'oraisa; but Tosafot hold it does not apply to it, rather even though it is d'rabbanan, nevertheless whatever the rabbis enacted etc.`,
  "beur-hagra:4:ד": `However etc. — as Ran wrote in Shabbat there that even for Shema if he began in a permitted state he need not interrupt, from what is written in chapter Eiruvin 96a we do not interrupt for Shabbatot; see Tosafot there 100a s.v. ein etc., and all the more per Rashbam s.v. ve-lo etc.; and although they say there the halacha is not etc., rather one spreads etc. because kiddush in place of a meal and before it a meal was enacted, but for other d'oraisa matters not; see siman 235 seif 2; and Ran follows his reasoning who wrote in Eiruvin on what is written there "and both agree there is no" etc., and if he brought etc. "and both" refers from darkening; but the latter "and if he brought" deals from daytime; and likewise in Milchamot in the name of Ra'avad; and likewise in Shulchan Aruch above siman 471 seif 2 specifically before the tenth hour — examine there`,
  "beur-hagra:5:_": `(ה) If etc. and was silent etc. — see Rosh chapter 1 of Berachot siman 14; Ra'avad challenged etc.; it appears etc.; unlike in such a case; therefore even per Tosafot there who ruled the doubt stringently — here he fulfilled; and on seif 3`,
  "beur-hagra:7:_": `(ז) If he forgot etc. — Menachot 72a: shortened by day is valid; and we taught etc.; and explained in Halakhot Gedolot like the anonymous view in Menachot; and Rabbenu Tam explained like the anonymous view in Megillah since it taught it with the fixed halacha; and as written in Bava Kamma 103 and in Nazir 6; and further we rule like R' Yishmael who pushes off Shabbat; and R' Gamliel and the court hold like him, and so R' Yochanan; therefore shortened by day is invalid; see Tosafot there 66a s.v. zachar; and in Megillah there wrote even for the anonymous view in Menachot counting is specifically at night since the verse repeated temimot; and wrote there as Shulchan Aruch wrote here`,
  "beur-hagra:8:א": `(ח) If he forgot. In Menachot there wrote in the name of Halakhot Gedolot that he should not count further and they wondered on him; and in Megillah wrote as Shulchan Aruch wrote because we require temimot`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ב": `But if. Some say counting nowadays is d'oraisa as above; and we say in chapter 3 of Berachot safeik omer emet ve-yatziv etc.; and although Tosafot in Menachot and Rosh hold it is d'rabbanan, nevertheless they wrote that even if certainly he did not count he counts on other nights, for each night is a mitzvah by itself; and all this per poskim who in rabbinic uncertainty bless — and see end of siman 375; see siman 67 and what is written; requires study`,
  "beur-hagra:9:א": `(ט) Shabbat night etc. and in what is written etc. — as written in chapter 8 of Berachot 92a and in Eiruvin 105b: it is different between entries etc.`,
  "beur-hagra:9:ג": `And when it falls. For the seventh does not fall on Motzei Shabbat; and on the second Yom Tov one does not sanctify in the synagogue as written in siman 487 seif 2 — see there`,
  "biur-halacha:1:ב": `To count the omer — behold Rambam and Chinuch hold it applies from the Torah even now; indeed Tur and Shulchan Aruch and several poskim hold it is not nowadays but a remembrance of the Temple when they offered the omer; and so is the sugya of poskim in this siman; indeed Rambam is not alone in his view, for R' Yeshaya likewise — see Shulchan Aruch Mechaber beginning siman 234; and R' Binyamin there end of siman — see there; and Ravyah brought in Or Zaru'a siman 329 — it is implied Or Zaru'a also concedes to his law — see there; and from words of Rav Yehudai and Rav Amram and Ritz Giat who agreed to their words brought in Ittur that if he did not count from evening he lost — we are strict on temimot — it appears from the Torah and not merely remembrance; Ittur rejected their words because counting the omer for us is not d'oraisa since now there is no offering nor sacrifice and no precision in temimot — it appears for them it is d'oraisa [one may say somewhat they hold although only remembrance of Temple the rabbis made it like d'oraisa specifically temimot]; therefore great support for our custom that we are careful not to count until stars emerge, since per many authorities it is d'oraisa; afterward I found similar in Avodat Yom Tov except he did not mention all these great ones we wrote`,
  "biur-halacha:1:ג": `And mitzvah on each one etc. — see Mishna Berurah what I wrote — dispute of Acharonim whether we say in this "one who hears is as one who speaks" — dispute of Levush and Chayei Adam with Peri Chadash; Levush and Chayei Adam like lulav we expound "and you shall take for yourselves" for each one — so too here each must count himself; Peri Chadash is lenient; and so inclines Birkei Yosef; in my opinion this is dispute among Rishonim: Rashi explained there Menachot 65b on what the Gemara says for each one — each is obligated to count; and in Chiddushei Rashba his words imply if Torah's intent is only the congregation and not each individual — it should say "and you shall count" alone and be silent; and since it says "to you" it implies to say to each one — see there; behold the mitzvah applies to each individual separately; against this early Ritz Giat implies one can fulfill another — there in laws of counting: "and we received from our teachers support for counting the omer standing from 'when the sickle begins in the standing grain' — you need not learn standing grain except standing; and on Motzei Shabbat they say after evening prayer and yihi noam and kedusha of the order while sitting — early ones practiced the prayer leader bless standing and all the congregation answer amen with intent not to trouble the congregation to return and stand" — end; likewise Orchot Chayim in name of R' Hai Gaon — see there; it implies they fulfill through his counting if their intent is they fulfill only through blessing alone — if so they must stand for counting itself which must be standing as above [in my opinion one should examine even per Rashi above where one is not expert to count himself — perhaps another can fulfill him and it is no worse than Birkat HaMazon where one cannot fulfill another per all unless he ate himself because it is written "and you shall eat and be satisfied" — whoever ate blesses; nevertheless we rule one who counts blesses and an ignoramus fulfills; perhaps so here — requires study]; behold we clarified this is dispute of authorities; therefore l'chatchila certainly each must count himself; b'dieved if he heard from another and intended to fulfill he returns and counts without blessing; so too Peri Megadim`,
  "biur-halacha:1:ד": `He counts and continues — see Acharonim who disputed if he counted in acronyms such as he said today two days etc. whether he fulfilled; Chayei Adam wrote since for most poskim counting nowadays is only d'rabbanan one should not be stringent from uncertainty and return to count without blessing. Know in any case if he blessed in his heart certainly he did not fulfill at all — thought is not like speech; so Peri Chadash explicitly in seif 7; see above siman 62 seif 4 in Mishna Berurah and Biur Halacha there — examine`,
  "biur-halacha:10:_": `Even in our time — see Mishna Berurah what is written we cannot protest those who are lenient — so many Acharonim; they sided finding reason for world custom as Mishna Berurah wrote; nevertheless since it is known many who are careful regarding any prohibition in other prohibitions are also lenient here — I saw the reason is they think whoever wants to be careful must be careful in all stringencies of guards and wages and also in purging vessels — difficult to be careful in these countries; therefore each relies on world custom to be entirely lenient; but in my opinion it is not proper because difficulty in all stringencies should not make entirely lenient; behold in flour it is easy to be careful as today found everywhere wheat flour sown in Cheshvan (and I heard from acquaintances good wheat flour sown in Poland are all sown in Cheshvan and those from Volhynia majority are old from those sown in Cheshvan); also from presumption possibly majority are old; and in beer several Acharonim side to leniency — see Penei Yehoshua and Mishkenot Yaakov and other Acharonim; also beer is not certainly new for even in winter months beer is sometimes made from old barley malt (and although many great Acharonim are stringent also on beer as explained Yoreh De'ah and Acharonim there — lenient have whom to rely on); in doubt we have Or Zaru'a who wrote one may rely in pressing circumstance on those who hold new grain d'rabbanan and it is rabbinic doubt; likewise in purging vessels and lees there are sides to leniency; but in the groats themselves of barley and oats in our country they are certainly new in winter; although we cannot protest world custom to be lenient as Acharonim wrote — proper and correct to be stringent for oneself at least in this; behold for great Rishonim it is d'oraisa — Rif, Rosh, Rambam, Semag, Ittur, Mordechai, in name of Ravyah, Hagahot Maimoniyot, Ritva; and even Or Zaru'a who sides d'rabbanan in chutz la-aretz did not rely to be lenient except in doubt — then one may rely in pressing circumstance on those who hold d'rabbanan and it is double doubt; but not certainly new; Terumat HaDeshen also wrote thus; Peskei Tosafot Menachot wrote of gentiles' grain it is d'rabbanan — if so at least d'rabbanan; nevertheless forbidden; we have no lenient ones except R' Baruch Baal HaTosafot who wrote to be lenient in distant places abroad; Ma'aleh Tzedek in name of R' Avigdor Cohen in name of Rivash wrote to be lenient entirely on gentiles' grain as brought in Bach — they are individual against all these authorities; also several lenient Acharonim did not wish to be lenient except in doubt not certainly; Shulchan Aruch here and Yoreh De'ah sealed like poskim it is d'oraisa in all respects; many Acharonim also grasped thus for essence; therefore certainly proper and correct to be concerned for all this and separate at least from certainly new as above; whoever wishes to give heart to this can obtain in winter from these species from old produce`,
  "biur-halacha:2:_": `And so is proper to do — see Mishna Berurah if he counted in twilight he fulfilled — so Magen Avraham and other Acharonim; this matter is mentioned in Tosafot Menachot 66 s.v. zachar when is it called twilight — see siman 261 that Shulchan Aruch holds there twilight is three-quarters mil before stars and is about a quarter hour approximately; before that is certainly day — see Biur Halacha s.v. ve-shiur end of Shulchan Aruch HaRav there that even for those who hold twilight begins immediately after sunset — for Shabbat we are stringent like R' Yehuda; but to be lenient even in rabbinic matters requires study whether we can be lenient and say two leniencies: one that it is twilight like R' Yehuda and also rabbinic uncertainty to leniency since for R' Yosi it is certainly day all the time from sunset until near stars — as we say in Gemara Shabbat 34 when twilight of R' Yehuda completes twilight of R' Yosi begins — requires study`,
  "biur-halacha:3:א": `While still day — see Mishna Berurah and it is implied from them that from plag ha-Minchah all agree he cannot count; know many Acharonim [Levush, Avodat Yom Tov, Chayei Adam, Maamar Mordechai, Nahar Shalom] explain "while still day" — from plag Minchah and above; and what he said he counts with them — because although by essential law since it is not yet night it is not time of counting as in previous seif — nevertheless places practiced leniency because they were accustomed to pray Maariv before dark and feared if they do not count then in congregation and each goes home he will forget from preoccupation and not count and the essence of counting Torah will be nullified; therefore in pressing circumstance one who says counting nowadays is only remembrance of Temple for most poskim one should not be more stringent than Shema and prayer; since they consider this start of night and read Shema and pray Maariv — likewise we should consider night for counting; and he says even a scholar if his heart troubles him lest he be preoccupied and forget to count privately — he may count with congregation but not bless then in any case by essential law it is not time of counting then — only we do not object to custom of those places and so they not be entirely nullified from this mitzvah; and when time arrives at night he blesses and counts — and it is not blessing in vain for first counting is nothing by essential law as above`,
  "biur-halacha:3:ג": `And he blesses and counts at night — see Mishna Berurah Peri Megadim wrote on erev Shabbat in congregation those who delay is better than those who precede and say already night and a moment before say great day to accept Shabbat — I call: heavens darkness to light and that light in brief moment to darkness — therefore proper to prevent this etc. — see there`,
  "biur-halacha:4:א": `If he says to him today such-and-such etc. — see Taz who brought proofs this is as intending not to fulfill; behold his proofs were rejected by Acharonim [see Avodat Yom Tov, Maamar Mordechai, Nahar Shalom]; nevertheless in essence of law one should examine well — Gra holds this seif is only per those who say mitzvot do not require intent; so Peri Chadash; for us who rule mitzvot require intent he must return and bless [and he does not hold like Radbaz to distinguish d'oraisa and d'rabbanan nor Chayei Adam's resolutions in s.k. 16 nor Magen Avraham s.k. 8]; further in my opinion if this was twilight and this respondent is always accustomed to bless specifically at stars emerging — it is reasonable in this manner he explicitly intends not to fulfill and he may return afterward and count with blessing; for practical law requires study`,
  "biur-halacha:6:_": `Or the reverse that they are four and he opened etc. and erred and concluded with five etc. — see Mishna Berurah Acharonim wondered on this; Taz resolved Mechaber does not mean he concluded on counting itself but on blessing — meaning at start of blessing when he said "Blessed are You, our God, King of the world" he thought he would count afterward four and only at conclusion of blessing it seemed to him the day is fifth of the omer and he concluded the blessing intending to count five of the omer — he does not return and bless, for we follow opening; and in first case Mechaber depicted similarly — there reason we follow conclusion and because it is rabbinic matter we follow both to leniency and he does not return and bless — Acharonim codified this law (see Darkhei Halacha and Avodat Yom Tov); but Taz derived new law: where opening and conclusion were with intent to count day 5 and after finishing blessing he learned the day is day 4 — he returns and blesses; in this law several Acharonim did not agree — see Mishna Berurah and Sha'arei Teshuvah`,
  "biur-halacha:8:ב": `Without blessing — Nahar Shalom wrote one whose deceased lies before him on one of the omer days counts by day after burial without blessing; and it is not similar to prayer one does not pray as makeup as in Yoreh De'ah siman 341 since essentially he was exempt; here per those who count it even by day it is not makeup for night but his obligation extends until end of the festival as implied Rambam chapter 7 Laws of Festivals; and similar to havdalah one makes havdalah on day 1 after burial of deceased whose essential time is until day 4; therefore one who counts by day can count remaining days with blessing; however if burial extended all day such that he had no leisure to count after burial — he should not count further days with blessing — what difference if he forgot to count or deceased prevented counting — either way not temimot — see there; what he decided for one obligated to count by day is not from makeup reason — see Or Zaru'a siman 329 what is written in name of Abba ha-Ezri; in essence of law what is ruled an onen is exempt — Rav Nuv'ei Mahadura Kama 8 Orach Chayim 27 is uncertain per Maharshal responsum 70 that not from all matters is onen exempt; he gathers some reasons one may say also from counting not exempt; primarily because he is not preoccupied and not nullified for this moment from engaging with deceased; he combines also view of some poskim onen exempt but permitted to fulfill; per all this his view he counts while deceased before him without blessing and again can count all days with blessing since without this some poskim do not require temimot at all — see there; Rav Akiva Eiger copied in his glosses`,
  "biur-halacha:8:ג": `He counts on remaining days with blessing — see Mishna Berurah what is written regarding if he erred in days etc. — Taz's words; although he only cited erred in days and not weeks — it is obvious likewise and all the more erred in weeks and not days since days impede more than weeks for all — as in Mishna Berurah s.k. 7; so Peri Megadim; nevertheless per reasoning it appeared Taz deals only with remaining weekdays that do not impede that he also mention days as Acharonim agreed [see Chayei Adam s.k. 8]; therefore one may say likewise if he erred in it — no worse than not mentioning at all; unlike day completing weeks where by law he must mention days and it impedes as s.k. 7; if so obvious likewise if he erred in counting days his counting is nothing and is as if entirely skipped and counts remaining days without blessing; possible Taz's view even on days completing weeks one may be lenient for upcoming countings since for many poskim days do not impede each other — if so at least one may be lenient when he mentioned one of the numbers — days or weeks; see Avodat Yom Tov who also questioned Taz's essential law — requires study for practical law; Peri Megadim also spoke on this and there are printing errors in his words and I could not understand his view`,
  "chatam-sofer:2:_": `(there in Magen Avraham s.k. 90) that the last day is always deferred — marginal note: and regarding temimot see Mishnah 4 chapter 4 of Sanhedrin and see in HaRa'avad and Tosefot Yom Tov there`,
  "chatam-sofer:3:_": `(there s.k. 1) One must count the day; therefore one does not bless — as it should read`,
  "chatam-sofer:4:_": `(there in Magen Avraham s.k. 2) He counts by himself — marginal note: and it is not similar to taking the lulav, for a mitzvah performed by one's body such as donning tefillin and taking the lulav — one cannot fulfill for another; unlike a mitzvah through speech, one who hears is as one who speaks — see Kapos Temarim Sukkah daf 41b; and that which we say "and you shall count" — the counting shall be for each one — is to exclude counting the Jubilee, which is only for the great court in Jerusalem, as Tosafot wrote Menachot daf 65b s.v. ve-sefaratem etc., see there; and see Chiddushei Sofer Orach Chayim siman 210`,
  "chatam-sofer:5:_": `(there in Taz s.k. 10) with havdalah and counted before kiddush — marginal note: Taz's reasoning is not so strong, for in kiddush he does not interrupt the sanctity of seven and it is not comparable to havdalah that separates between the sanctity of Shabbat and the sanctity of Yom Tov. And examine. It appears to me by reasoning that one should precede counting the omer, per what Magen Avraham s.k. 1 wrote that the counting is not a contradiction; and further since we are expert in fixing the month, counting is primary — therefore one blesses; and for this reason there is indeed a contradiction, only that perforce one must bless; and one may say that in any case one should precede counting, which is by law, and not precede kiddush, for he has already taken hold of Yom Tov — and then counting is said in contradiction; unlike if he precedes counting, then kiddush, which is custom, is said in contradiction and we are not so concerned, as above — examine. But Chayei Adam already wrote the custom is to sanctify first; see in Peri Megadim`,
  "chatam-sofer:6:_": `In two orders as well — marginal note: see Tosafot Avodah Zarah beginning of chapter Ein Ma'amidin, Rosh beginning of chapter Kol HaBasar`,
  "chatam-sofer:7:_": `(there s.k. 17) and Mishna Berurah holds — marginal note: for R' Baruch, so it should read`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:א": `Evening prayer. They say the Maariv prayers belonging to the second night even if the first day falls on Shabbat; for on other festivals if the first day falls on Shabbat they say on the second night the Maariv of the first day and the second is deferred (and as Magen Avraham wrote so is primary in Gemara end Sukkah in what we say there "let them fall, let it be deferred" unlike Pozna custom); nevertheless on the second day of Pesach they always say the Maariv belonging to that day because of the firstborn for counting; and so is practiced`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ב": `They begin to count the omer. That they bless on counting the omer on Yom Tov and we are not concerned lest they come to treat lightly the second day of Yom Tov — for from what is written one does not bless on the sukkah on the eighth day lest they come to treat lightly and say this is not the eighth but the seventh which is the fifth day; behold they bless on the sukkah — if so here too lest they treat lightly and say it is the fifth day since they bless on counting and not doubt of day 1, for it is written "from the morrow of the Shabbat you shall count" — from the morrow of Yom Tov; see Magen Avraham what Kesef Mishneh wrote resolves this question; see in Tamim De'im siman 291 in Ra'avad's hasagot and Ran chapter Lulav and Aravah and Maharshal responsum 68 who wrote other resolutions; see in Taz end siman 249; and one may further distinguish there we are concerned lest they treat lightly and say it is still the fifth day seventh of Sukkot since they bless to sit in sukkah — unlike here we are not concerned lest they treat lightly and say it is the fifth day and do melacha on it, for he did not make havdalah on first Yom Tov — first day prohibition still applies all who did not make havdalah; and we are not so concerned lest they also make havdalah against enactment of Sages and custom — requires study`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ד": `To count for himself. Language of Agudah in Menachot "and you shall count for yourselves" — counting shall be for each one and not prayer leader on behalf of all; and so is implication of Gemara and Rishonim; and Rashba responsum 126 explicitly; and although one who hears is as one who speaks — this is only regarding blessing as Rav wrote seif 3 in gloss — and this is Rashba's words in responsum Beit Yosef brought; but counting is diminished from Scripture explicitly "and you shall count" shall be counting for each one; as we expound "and you shall take" regarding lulav taking shall be for each — so here; and Tosefta chapter Lulav HaGazul implies; although most poskim hold counting nowadays d'rabbanan — nevertheless like d'oraisa was enacted and it is obvious from implication of poskim; so Levush and Magen Mishneh; but in end he brought in name of Rashba Beit Yosef brought that even through prayer leader's counting one fulfills — this is not Rashba's intent only on blessing as he wrote explicitly responsum 127; and what Beit Yosef wrote on prayer leader's counting is not precise as it implies and clear from beginning and end of his words — see there Beit Yosef; and clear explicitly Rav's implication nearby seif 3 in gloss — Rashba's words in Beit Yosef — on blessing it refers`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ו": `And to bless. That they bless on this counting and not on counting of niddah and zavah — Radbaz responsum 27 wrote since counting seven clean days is not obligatory mitzvah — if she did not wish to cleave to husband and enter Temple and eat kodshim but remain impure, permission is in her hand — therefore she does not bless on her counting — end. It is difficult: if so how does she bless on immersion; and on slaughter; and similar many blessings; rather since in any case when she wishes to immerse for husband she must count and immerse — why not bless on it; further, for those who say (Yoma 88a) immersion in its time is mitzvah — what is there to say; therefore it appears to me primary reason of Rishonim as Tosafot Ketubot 72b and other poskim: because if she sees she will retroactively invalidate — therefore she does not bless; and even per poskim on private fast they say its matter in morning although possible accident will nullify fast — Radbaz explained there nevertheless it is fast of hours, unlike niddah whose nullification is complete — end; in my opinion it is not difficult anyway — accident is not common, unlike blood common in woman full of blood — requires study`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ח": `And he counts days and weeks. If he counted days alone and did not mention weeks — Acharonim agree he fulfills b'dieved; if he counted weeks and not days — Magen Avraham wrote he fulfills as Tur wrote; his intent as Abba ha-Ezri wrote on day eight he says only today one week and one day; and all the more on day eight days he already counted in past week; and on day 9 until reaching 14 he says today fourteen days which are two weeks; on day 15 today two weeks and one day; and always — end; unlike if on day 7 or 14 he did not mention days — possibly he did not fulfill; so nephew of Rav in Sha'arei Tzedek in name of R' Yitzchak; see Mishna Berurah first edition brought two views if he counted days in acronyms such as today fourteen days — some say did not fulfill and some fulfilled; in my opinion all similar should return and count without blessing since per consensus of most poskim counting days and weeks is only d'rabbanan nowadays; see Magen Avraham wrote one must count specifically in language he understands what he says — if not it is not counting at all`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ט": `Today day one of the omer. Consensus of Acharonim not to say "that today"; and one who says "that today" did not err and has whom to rely — Rokeach and baraita and Maharal; printed thus in Pesach Me'ubin; interpretation must be: word "that today" gives taste to what he blesses now on counting omer because today is such-and-such days; likewise regarding nusach "in the omer" Rav and Taz expanded reason not to say "to the omer"; but in truth most old nuschaot wrote to say "to the omer" as in Kol Bo and baraita and Shulchan Lechem and Rokeach and Rashba responsum and Beit Yosef and Ran; must be word "to the omer" implies to the day of the omer such-and-such days; unlike "in the omer" implies now is time of omer — in truth time of omer is only day 1 — 16 Nisan — requires study; so Shelah that one should say "to the omer" specifically; nevertheless do not change from custom practiced from olden days for they have whom to rely on`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:י": `Until he reaches fourteen days. Maharash Kaidenbar responsum 27 wrote l'chatchila one should count the smaller number first per R' Yehuda Yoma 55; in places counting larger number first per R' Meir — count per local custom — end; now we count smaller number first. Shulchan Lechem siman 73 until ten say days and from eleven on say day — this is elegant Hebrew language — end`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:כ": `Until 49 days. Tosafot Megillah 20b s.v. kol halailah wrote saying after counting "may it be His will" etc. to build the Temple etc. — since it is only remembrance to build Temple; but on shofar and lulav there is action — end of his words. Maharil wrote when reaching day 49 he says they are seven weeks and the next day is erev Shavuot — end: practiced to say Psalm "May God grant us grace" — so Magen Avraham; new ones came near and printed counting nusach according to secret and intention of sefirot; and similar in encirclement of lulav they say verses in order of Ari's intentions; and printed order of prayer with Avodat HaBoreh; and many collected kabbalistic matters spread this in eyes of the multitude as if whoever wishes may take the Name; in truth it is not fit for a fool — honor, for honor of God is hidden matter; I copy Maharshal responsum 98: I saw many punctilious in poverty to leave tefillin of arm while sitting — know this is new ones come near wishing to be of kabbalists and hidden midrashim and weak-sighted — do not gaze at Zohar light nor know its source and intent but found thus in books of Rabbeinu Bachaye; know all my teachers and holy fathers who served geonim of the world did not practice thus but words of Talmud poskim; now open your eyes and see if there was substance — impossible no author mentioned; also no hint in Talmud Bavli and Yerushalmi, Sifra, Pesikta, She'iltot; whoever did not know to grasp its secret properly — come to cut saplings; therefore my beloved do not walk their path; you have no business in hidden matters boasting in innovations as if they know and understand secrets of Torah and its treasuries — would they knew the revealed; how fine is power of R' Shimon bar Yochai who after learning kabbalah books said he prays like a one-day-old infant etc. — therefore practice your way and let them have theirs; but know all Mishnah — its hand below and their hand above — end Maharshal; from his words one should not change custom even among kabbalists; all the more other custom changes mentioned in new books; and even if one says permitted to change something for certain individuals renowned and established in piety — knowers of charm if they wish to intend something or change on their own what they found innovation in Acharonim' words — also in concealment and not publicize matter to change custom; they shout like peddlers on whoever does like old custom as if Torah innovations from them — God knows if for mockery; see siman 68 Magen Avraham in name of intentions: customs practiced in prayer roots one should not change from local custom for twelve gates in heaven against twelve tribes and each tribe has gate and custom alone; what is mentioned in Gemara is equal for all — Yerushalmi: although we sent you prayer order do not change from custom of your fathers — end`,
  "chok-yaakov:10:א": `To eat new grain. Likewise liquids emerging from them as in chapter Or VeRotev 104 — tevel and new — liquids emerging from them like themselves; consensus of Rishonim to forbid from Torah beer made from new; Rambam explicitly chapter 10 Laws of Forbidden Foods law 22; Rokeach siman 294; all poskim who toiled finding permission for beer nowadays — from this they hold liquids like themselves per this baraita; all unlike Shakh Yoreh De'ah 293 in name of Lechem Mishneh on mishnayot straining to say we rule liquid emerging is permitted — his words have no foundation and essence per halacha consensus in Gemara and Rishonim; Shakh himself there also Beit Hillel — see there. Main permission relied upon contradicts all great Rishonim — Rif, Rambam, Rosh, Tur, Kol Bo, Rokeach, Semak, Semag who held forbidden d'oraisa new grain nowadays — must be for reason Rama Yoreh De'ah 293 in name of Rishonim; Rashba siman 259; aggadah chapter 1 Kiddushin we rely on majority grain old or rooted before omer (even in majority not before us we follow latter matter of Torah — see Rashba responsum 120; even if country grains not rooted before omer gates of countries not locked — end): Shelah wrote in Poland one should be careful in barley, oats, spelt not brought there from other countries and majority all sow after Pesach — end; Lekutei Maharil wrote in year 5576 small leap year they delay sowing until after Pesach — be careful — see there he expanded; nevertheless I did not see careful even one in a thousand pious people; they rely on Maharil and his collections that need not be careful since poskim hold new in chutz la-aretz d'rabbanan; Terumat HaDeshen 291 in name of Or Zaru'a; Magen Avraham; now Sages did not forbid at all since we are distant from Eretz Yisrael — see there; further one may side to permit because Bach Yoreh De'ah 293 wrote new grain does not apply to gentiles' grain although against Yerushalmi as Tosefta chapter 1 Kiddushin wrote and against poskim; Rosh responsum general 2:1 likewise Semak; Bach's proofs from there our custom already rejected in Berakhat HaZevach 137a; Shakh and Taz there Yoreh De'ah; Taz concluded there so is view of some Rishonim; Peskei Tosafot Menachot R' Eliezer of Ivra — see there; combined with pressing time as Taz there — therefore practiced to be lenient`,
  "chok-yaakov:10:ב": `Even in our time d'oraisa. Halacha follows anonymous mishnah end Orlah; Rambam chapter 10 Laws of Forbidden Foods; what challenges this apparently from sugya chapter Kol HaKorbanot and Rambam chapter 7 Laws of Daily Offerings that he ruled one does not bring offerings from chutz la-aretz — already resolved there everything in Lechem Mishneh — see there — and it is correct`,
  "chok-yaakov:2:_": `They return to count when it darkens. If he counted in uncertain darkness he fulfilled b'dieved; per some poskim even l'chatchila one may count in uncertain darkness — see Beit Yosef and Avodat Yom Tov wrote from plag Minchah and above he returns and counts without blessing; Bach wrote and world custom now to bless in uncertain darkness; but before uncertain darkness obviously one does not bless even on Motzei Shabbat and Yom Tov — unlike Hagahot Minhagim`,
  "chok-yaakov:3:א": `And even if he answers amen on congregation's blessing. For congregation blesses while still day although counting time at least in uncertain darkness and not before — per all, nevertheless congregation blesses while still day to fulfill amei ha-aretz who do not count at night as Levush wrote; they rely as they rely in evening prayer and Shema read from daytime and rely — sunset is called night as explained above siman 235 — see there Magen Avraham s.k. 3; so here regarding counting d'rabbanan; and I found explicitly Peskei Tosafot Menachot siman 176: some say after they prayed evening prayer since it is night for Shema and prayer it is also night for omer — end; therefore congregation relies on this view; but one who wishes to be careful for himself counts in his time; thereby resolved what I saw in responsum R' Shlomo Levi siman 7 who challenged Beit Yosef contradicting himself and strained to resolve; per Lechem Mishneh Taz also strained to explain to extract poskim's words from simplicity; in truth as written all is correct and obvious; our custom not to count even in congregation while still day even on Motzei Shabbat and Yom Tov unless at least uncertain darkness as in nearby s.k. 12 in name of Bach`,
  "chok-yaakov:3:ב": `That he not fulfill. Meaning in blessing; but in counting in any case he is obligated to count for himself as above s.k. 4; Magen Avraham wrote; specifically when his intent was not to fulfill; but stam it implies he should not bless again from doubt since some say mitzvot do not require intent — so Avodat Yom Tov; but Taz's implication s.k. 16 is not so — see nearby s.k. 16`,
  "chok-yaakov:4:א": `To return and count with blessing. Although he did not say "in the omer" b'dieved he fulfilled — so Magen Avraham; Tur, Rokeach, Maharil did not require even l'chatchila to say "in the omer" — therefore need not return and count with blessing; although we rule mitzvot require intent (see Beit Yosef what Rashba wrote) as explained above siman 60 — nevertheless one should not bless again here as Magen Avraham s.k. 14; Biur Halacha possible this is called intent since he intends at least to say how many days of counting and his mitzvah is only speech; see Taz s.k. 16 whose words are very astonishing in what he explains Shulchan Aruch's words imply opposite — requires study`,
  "chok-yaakov:4:ב": `And when time arrives forbidden to eat until he counts. Nevertheless he must pray first evening prayer and Shema in its time which is frequent and before counting; so Peskei Tosafot I wrote s.k. 13 (Shelah wrote where there is group praying Maariv in its time proper to join them on omer days for then mitzvah draws mitzvah — Shema in time and counting in congregation — end). Therefore pleasing why Rav did not write half hour before time also forbidden to eat as explained above siman 232 and 235; per Lechem Mishneh pleasing it is not for need — half hour before otherwise forbidden because of prayer and Shema in time anyway as siman 235; but he needed to inform us even if he already prayed and read Shema in time nevertheless he should not eat since counting time arrived; all this appears obvious and clear; Maharil unnecessarily toiled dividing into various divisions for this question. Acharonim wrote Maharil did not eat on omer days evening meal until after stars emerge — first count`,
  "chok-yaakov:6:_": `He does not return and bless. Bach and other Acharonim challenged this law from above siman 59 and 298 did not fulfill since he spoke falsely; as poskim agreed similarly above siman 298 unlike Rambam; and so as not to equate Rishonim' words with complete error — I would distinguish: unlike above where cup of wine and concluded with wine did not fulfill; here since he blessed with intent for this day and when concluding said today day etc. — if so his intent was this day he stood in only erred in calculation of day; but essence of his blessing and counting was on day he stood in that he said today etc.; but there he concluded entirely on species not in his hand — cup of wine in hand and blessed on wine or concluded in Maariv "who forms light" — found he did not bless entirely on thing he wishes to bless; not evident matter unlike here he said today and it is evident error — requires study; therefore I would not place oneself in blessing doubt l'chatchila; for this Bach whom we follow; Rav also did not emend anything`,
  "chok-yaakov:9:א": `Before havdalah. After Kaddish titkabel — so Abudraham; and on Motzei Shabbat also count immediately after Kaddish titkabel; even per our custom saying ve-yiten lekha before havdalah — nevertheless count immediately after Kaddish titkabel before ve-yiten lekha since prayer already departed with Kaddish — mitzvah of counting applies; so practiced; for what Abudraham and customs wrote before havdalah — per their custom making havdalah immediately after Kaddish titkabel — unlike our custom; and that they count before havdalah although still Shabbat — for melacha; nevertheless Shabbat day already passed — so Magen Avraham s.k. 6; also already made havdalah in prayer`,
  "chok-yaakov:9:ב": `And when last Yom Tov of Pesach etc. But second day of Pesach one does not sanctify at all in synagogue as above siman 487; in their homes always count before kiddush because forbidden to eat before counting — so Magen Avraham's intent though printing error in his words; Taz wrote: it appears on last Yom Tov night always count before kiddush of last Yom Tov for better to draw upon it sanctity of seventh day which is from Torah and sanctity of last Yom Tov is d'rabbanan — end; against Rav's implication that always count after kiddush unless falls on Motzei Shabbat; and so practiced; possible for reasons explained Taz s.k. 2 — examine`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:1:_": `Seif 8: But if he is uncertain whether he skipped one day and did not count, he counts on the remaining days with a blessing. Marginal note: see the source of this law in Terumat HaDeshen and its reason and rationale there; and see in Chiddushei Halachot Berachot siman 295, and it is in my works on Orach Chayim from the year 5637 in responsum to the community of Michanovka near Barditchev on whether one blesses on s'chach — examine well`,
  "eshel-avraham:1:_": `He counts and continues. If he said "today forty minus one of the omer" he fulfilled — see Turei Zahav end of siman 10`,
  "eshel-avraham:8:_": `Blessing. And if he wrote on that day the days of counting that he wrote "today such-and-such days and weeks of the omer" he does not fulfill — for it is not like speech — see Turei Zahav what is written siman 20; and if he was an onen at night he counts after burial without blessing, and the night after — it is obvious he blesses — see Devar Moshe siman 33 and Yoreh De'ah siman 228 and Yad Efraim siman 3731`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:א": `(א) On the second night — Acharonim wrote they say Maariv belonging to day 2 even if second night falls on Motzei Shabbat; and although on other festivals if first day falls on Shabbat they say on second night Maariv of day 1 — here is different for Maariv is matter of counting`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:ה": `(ה) To count for himself — as written "and you shall count for yourselves" — mitzvah applies to each individual; it is implied from this in counting unlike other mitzvot dependent on speech regarding kiddush and havdalah etc. — if he heard another and intended to fulfill he fulfills because one who hears is as one who speaks; here Torah revealed he does not fulfill however much he did not count himself; but some Acharonim wrote Torah's intent is only it should not be said this mitzvah stands for court alone like Shemitta and Yovel where it says "and you shall count for yourself" — refers to congregation; but in truth if he heard from another who counted and intended to fulfill and also other intended to fulfill him — he fulfilled as everywhere we rule one who hears is as one who speaks; all this in counting; but in blessing they bless on counting — all agree possible to fulfill through another even if expert as in all blessings; nevertheless custom in all Israel each blesses and counts for himself and does not rely on prayer leader. Acharonim wrote counting is permitted in any language provided he understands that language; if he does not understand even if he counted in holy tongue he did not fulfill — since he did not know what he said it is not counting`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:ז": `(ז) And he counts days and weeks — as written "you shall count fifty days" and "seven weeks you shall count for yourself"; if he counted days alone and did not mention weeks — some say he fulfilled b'dieved and some say he must return and count days and weeks properly and therefore returns and counts without blessing [and if he forgot to return and count he counts remaining days with blessing]; if he counted weeks and not days such as on seventh day he said today one week and did not mention days at all — all agree he did not fulfill`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:ח": `(ח) In the omer — and most poskim nusach "to the omer"; however essence of this is only l'chatchila to clarify he counts from day they offered omer onward; if he only said today such-and-such he also fulfilled`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:י": `(י) And in this manner etc. — custom to say after counting "may it be His will" etc. to build Temple etc. — meaning then we fulfill mitzvah of bringing omer; some also practice saying Psalm "May God grant us grace" etc.`,
  "mishnah-berurah:10:א": `(מד) Forbidden to eat new grain — meaning grain rooted after 16 Nisan which is time of offering omer; if rooted before omer the omer permits as in Yoreh De'ah 293 — see there; further new grain applies only to five species wheat barley oats rye spelt`,
  "mishnah-berurah:10:ב": `(מה) Even in our time — as written "and bread and parched grain and groats you shall not eat until this very day" etc. "in all your dwellings" — implies even outside land prohibition applies until essence of day which is day of offering sacrifice 16 Nisan; because of uncertainty of day we are further stringent one more day; in Yoreh De'ah 293 Rama wrote permission for new grain — double doubt: perhaps grain is from previous year; and if this year perhaps rooted before omer; likewise if place bringing grain from places whose grain rooted before omer; Acharonim wrote in Poland one should not be lenient except wheat and rye for majority certainly sown in Cheshvan and new grain does not apply [unless those known sown in summer]; but barley oats spelt majority and almost all sown after Pesach; also not customary to bring grain there from other countries — do not be lenient in them; nevertheless majority of world not careful at all on new grain prohibition; some found merit because difficult to be careful — therefore rely in pressing circumstance on some Rishonim who hold new in chutz la-aretz only d'rabbanan because Sages decreed due to Eretz Yisrael and did not decree except places adjacent to Eretz Yisrael like Egypt and Bavel; some found merit holding new applies only to Israelite grain not gentiles' — therefore must warn Israelites who have grain sown in their fields to observe new grain prohibition; although we cannot protest lenient ones — nevertheless every person of spirit should not rely on these permissions and be stringent for himself in all possible; know further although Acharonim wrote regarding wheat no concern at all perhaps new since majority certainly sown in Cheshvon as today common to bring via railroad wheat flour from distant places; known in inner Russia many places sown in summer and there new grain is found almost more than old — if one knows flour comes from there must be careful in winter months when flour made from new grain; regarding beer and other new grain laws see Yoreh De'ah 293 and Acharonim there`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:א": `(יב) They return etc. — Rashba's words responsum 254; explained there congregation also prayed Maariv before; regarding Maariv they should not return and pray because of congregation trouble (as above beginning siman 483 seif 14) — only regarding counting we are not concerned for their trouble lest he say for example ten days when only nine for that day is ninth not tenth until stars — end there`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ג": `(יד) And the punctilious — meaning by law it was possible to be lenient to count from darkening even before stars since twilight is rabbinic uncertainty and we follow leniency in rabbinic uncertainty in counting nowadays d'rabbanan for most poskim; nevertheless not proper to place oneself in uncertainty l'chatchila — therefore punctilious wait until stars which is certainly night`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ד": `(טו) And so is proper — meaning l'chatchila; nevertheless b'dieved if he blessed in twilight he fulfilled as above; Avodat Yom Tov questions and sides proper he return and count at stars without blessing`,
  "mishnah-berurah:3:א": `(טז) While still day — some Acharonim explained meaning twilight which is rabbinic uncertainty and they fulfill counting then for poskim who hold counting nowadays d'rabbanan; he wishes to be punctilious and count from stars as above; Mechaber says he counts with them lest he forget afterward and think in his mind if I remember at night to count I do not wish to fulfill with this counting — as in gloss below; therefore when time arrives afterward he blesses and counts; see Biur Halacha`,
  "mishnah-berurah:4:א": `(יט) Twilight etc. — he specified twilight because b'dieved if he blessed in twilight he fulfilled as above; and all the more if asked him after stars he must be careful not to answer today such-and-such`,
  "mishnah-berurah:4:ג": `(כא) He cannot etc. — although he did not say "in the omer" he fulfilled b'dieved as above s.k. 8`,
  "mishnah-berurah:4:ד": `(כב) To return and count with blessing — since he already fulfilled counting per those who say mitzvot do not require intent; although we rule they require intent and this one did not intend to fulfill this mitzvah — for blessing one must be stringent and be concerned for that view and not bless on counting he will count afterward; there is further advice to explicitly intend not to fulfill with this counting. Acharonim wrote deals if asked him before reaching weeks; if asked at hour reaching weeks even if said today such-and-such it is nothing if he did not mention weeks — must return and count with blessing`,
  "mishnah-berurah:4:ז": `(כה) He stops and counts — per those who hold counting nowadays d'oraisa as in gloss; if so for us who hold d'rabbanan he does not stop — as above siman 232 regarding Minchah; some Acharonim wrote therefore Rama here in our matter was concerned to be stringent like that view since it is matter without any trouble to interrupt briefly and count`,
  "mishnah-berurah:5:ב": `(כט) And concluded like it he fulfilled — meaning even in blessing if he opened blessing with this intent and concluded; there is conclusion and opening; nevertheless l'chatchila he should not bless unless he knows from start which day it is — forbidden to interrupt even in silence between blessing and mitzvah more than kedei dibbur`,
  "mishnah-berurah:6:ג": `(לב) He does not return and bless — Acharonim wondered on this law; granted initially concluding with five when it is five is fine he counted properly although at time of blessing he thought four — nothing since even without blessing at all he fulfilled; but in latter case what helps us that at time of blessing he knew which day — nevertheless in his mouth he counted wrong day; they agreed in this he must count anew and bless for first blessing was in vain since he concluded after it different day unless he remembered within kedei dibbur of his counting then he returns and counts properly and blessing counts; know further although Shulchan Aruch deals when at time of blessing he knew day of counting and blessed intending to count properly but afterward erred and concluded improperly; several Acharonim agreed even if at time of blessing his mind was also on different day and he erred in counting — nevertheless if within kedei dibbur he remembered he erred he immediately concludes properly and fulfills thus; further they wrote if he erred and said today day four of omer and within twenty-four he remembered it is day five — suffices he conclude fifth in omer and fulfills thus even if he did not say today day five since still within kedei dibbur`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ב": `(לו) He counts on other days — per many poskim days do not impede each other; each day and day is mitzvah by itself`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ד": `(לח) He counts remaining days with blessing — there is double doubt: perhaps he did not skip at all; and if skipped perhaps halacha follows those poskim each day is mitzvah by itself; likewise in any matter whose law is to return and count without blessing from doubt — if he did not return and count he counts remaining nights with blessing [Acharonim]; therefore if erred in days and not weeks or erred in weeks and not days — law is return and count without blessing as above s.k. 7; if he did not return and count nevertheless he counts remaining days with blessing; see Biur Halacha`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:א": `(לט) After kiddush in synagogue — all we can advance sanctity of day we advance; if he counts at home he counts before kiddush for forbidden to eat before counting as above`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:ד": `(מב) And when last Yom Tov etc. — therefore specified last Yom Tov for seventh of Pesach does not fall on Motzei Shabbat; on second night of Pesach one does not sanctify in synagogue as siman 487 [Gra and Chayei Adam]`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:ה": `(מג) One should count before — as above to exclude we defer day like other matters as much as possible; although here he accepts Yom Tov sanctity in his kiddush nevertheless Shabbat sanctity is greater than Yom Tov sanctity; Taz's view not specifically when last Yom Tov on Motzei Shabbat — likewise even on weekday one should precede counting to kiddush for better to defer sanctity of seventh day from Torah than accept last Yom Tov sanctity which is only d'rabbanan; but Acharonim disagreed`,
  "netiv-chayim:1:_": `(Magen Avraham s.k. 1) Kesef Mishnah challenged — see: Ran wrote at the end of Pesachim and at the end of Sukkah`,
  "peri-megadim:1:א": `One begins — Taz brought Ran's words end of Eiruvin. And Tosafot Menachot 65b s.v. ve-sefaratem wrote that in Jubilee the court counts and blesses; and a zavah lest she see and become impure — see there; nevertheless one may say a mitzvah for a zavah to count orally without blessing; and we wrote Ran ruled; and this is "and you shall count" — mitzvah on each one; it is implied mitzvah is, unlike other countings there is no mitzvah; Avodat Yom Tov and Chayei Adam 6: what they bless on slaughter lest one find treifah — majority are valid — see Yoreh De'ah 19. Radbaz explained there is no obligation that if she does not cleave to her husband she need not count seven clean days; Chayei Adam challenged how she blesses on immersion — see there; one may say counting seven clean days is not completion of mitzvah and not obligatory, unlike immersion and counting the omer which is obligatory; and all the more each night is a mitzvah by itself per one view`,
  "peri-megadim:10:_": `After — Taz: behold in Shulchan Aruch specifically when last Yom Tov on Motzei Shabbat — on weekday counting after kiddush; Taz by his reasoning disagrees and is stringent even on weekday; see Terumat HaDeshen 4 that on weekday one counts after kiddush — see there; Chayei Adam sign 21; and so is practiced when it falls on weekday on the eighth day they count after kiddush. What Taz wrote if he erred in counting it is as if he did not count at all and counts other days without blessing — unlike if he erred in days and not weeks he counts other days with blessing — Magen Avraham 4; and implies all the more erred in weeks and not days; implies even if it was day 14 and he erred and said 17 or 15 and concluded they are two weeks — he fulfilled; requires slight study — one may say specifically when it was 14 and he erred and said 13 or 15. What Mechaber wrote in seif 8 if he forgot to bless on one of the days — meaning he did not count — if he counted without blessing it does not prevent and he counts other days with blessing; see Levush and Tosafot Menachot 66a s.v. zachar`,
  "peri-megadim:2:א": `And mitzvah — Taz Menachot 65b 71a; and there it will be explained; Avodat Yom Tov 1; and one may say "and you shall count" implies all Israel; "to you" — in Israel through hearing from the prayer leader, unlike Jubilee where it is written "and you shall count for yourself" — the court; also in Jubilee we rule even the court has no mitzvah to count. Peri Chadash 8 brought a question: one who forgot day 1 to count — may he be prayer leader and count with blessing to fulfill others and he too fulfills? He raised he cannot, for he is not obligated in the matter; proof from Megillah: a city dweller does not fulfill for a metropolis dweller; likewise in 488 and 489 — see there; but he may hear the blessing from another and intend to fulfill through his blessing — see there. Behold certainly although it is a matter of mitzvah, whoever is not obligated in that mitzvah does not fulfill others — Yoreh De'ah and Tur from the reason of arevut; specifically deaf-mute and imbecile have arevut among them; for in siman 489 seif 2 a deaf person who speaks and does not hear — although it is a matter of mitzvah he does not fulfill others in shofar — Yoreh De'ah 120 there; see Rosh Hashana 29a; see in Rosh Berachot 82b who challenged whether women in Birkat HaMazon d'rabbanan do not fulfill men — Yoreh De'ah; and what is written from eating an olive-size that one fulfills others — he explained there from arevut and a woman is not in arevut; and resolve that if not, a woman is in arevut but not obligated in Birkat HaMazon — therefore arevut applies to her; one may say here too he says arevut does not apply to her for she is not obligated in this mitzvah; and likewise women in kiddush d'oraisa fulfill men in kiddush for they share arevut; Parshat Nitzavim Rashi there; and if not, a man for a woman — although he fulfilled he fulfills, for they are in arevut; see siman 271 seif 2 and 273; and in other places we wrote from this; and in opening it includes also adult for minor — Baruch She-amar 1: if he fulfilled he does not fulfill them, for arevut does not apply; and kiddush and Birkat HaMazon the rabbis enacted to fulfill his sons and household although he fulfilled, for they rely on him or obligated is called upon him to educate them; and likewise a woman similarly who is not obligated to educate her son — see Shulchan Aruch Magen Avraham 1`,
  "peri-megadim:2:ג": `Further I will explain to you what Peri Chadash wrote — proof from kiddush and return of prayer that the enactment did not move from its place; see siman 6 Taz 5 and Magen Avraham 7 whether the prayer leader blesses "who has redeemed" again — see there; and Tur wrote in siman 224 anonymous one repeats the prayer; and in 489 wrote one does not sanctify now in the synagogue; and in 488 blessing me'ein sheva — see there; Avodat Yom Tov there implies one should not distinguish between them; and for Tur one may say in 224 because of sanctity; and for Mechaber it is difficult; and one should not expand; and there in seif 7 and 8 I will explain further`,
  "peri-megadim:4:_": `One week — Taz Parshat Vayetzei; and in masculine form siman 7 remember inverted other words; Avodat Yom Tov Even HaEzer 126 sign 9: therefore "seven weeks" masculine; 16 masculine; unlike "seven Shabbatot temimot" Vayikra 23 — Shabbat feminine like mechaleleha etc. kodesh hi shemot 41; and refers to Shabbatot; on Shabbat day one says the week thus; and that which is written "the doer" melacha — Tosafot Ketubot 5a; siman 292:2 Taz; one may say on it refers (Shemot 31); and Rashi on Chumash "week of this" and refers to it; and likewise Rashbam and Ra'avad and Targumim; nevertheless "week" in Shulchan Aruch shtayna not week in kamatz; and two things he said — see there: week of this and also the word week here — sevenths of days shtayna etc. I built per masculine form siman 7 remember that the heh does not rest in the middle etc.: eight for masculine the nun kamatz and for feminine the nun segol eight amot; and in other matters inverted achot yaft in kamatz ish yafeh in segol; and what Mechaber wrote four days — see Avodat Yom Tov until ten days and from there on day; siman 226; Avodat Yom Tov 6; and so is practiced to say eleven days`,
  "peri-megadim:5:_": `Until — Avodat Yom Tov and Ran; language is shortened here and it should read: most commentators wrote that nowadays d'rabbanan — therefore better to count in twilight that temimot be certain; and rabbinic uncertainty to leniency, unlike Temple times required certainly uncertainty of Torah; Ran wrote it is not proper to make rabbinic uncertainty l'chatchila (and see 466 Magen Avraham 2); and if temimot one should not be stringent nowadays more than d'oraisa that by necessity one counts at stars. Tosafot Menachot 67a s.v. zachar and Tosafot Kerisot 17b s.v. from the latter that in twilight it is established somewhat; and in Lechem Mishneh chapter 8 inadvertent 2. One may distinguish between them; and for halacha l'chatchila he should not count until stars; if he counted in twilight he counts afterward without blessing — so appears chapter 7 matzah 4 cups 4 — rabbinic uncertainty since no trouble — so too here; and in chapter Nishaley — see in Magen Avraham 6:7 I will explain further. On erev Shabbat in congregation those who delay is better than those who precede and say already night and a moment before say great day to accept Shabbat — I call: heavens darkness to light and that light in a brief moment to darkness — therefore proper to prevent this; holy Israel hear and accept rebuke and reproof — therefore for accepting Shabbat they precede and for counting they delay`,
  "peri-megadim:7:_": `If — Taz: behold Mechaber in siman 60 seif 4 ruled mitzvot require intent in d'oraisa and d'rabbanan; one may say no intent is required — Magen Avraham 3; and counting nowadays is d'rabbanan for most poskim — see sign 9; if so one may say no intent is required; and Mishna Berurah wrote in seif 3 if his intent was not to fulfill — in stam he fulfilled — no intent required in d'rabbanan; nevertheless this that he answers today such-and-such — presumably only to answer the asker words; but his intent not to fulfill; nevertheless what brings proof from Birkat HaMazon siman 484 is difficult for it is d'oraisa; and from lulav 298 fine for other days d'rabbanan; Avodat Yom Tov since the adult does not hold the lulav — see there; Magen Avraham signs 8 and 9; and it implies for halacha b'dieved if he said today such-and-such he counts afterward with blessing — stam as his explanation that he intends not to fulfill; Peri Chadash and Chayei Adam 15: Mechaber's wording "he cannot" return and count with blessing implies forbidden to bless — as Magen Avraham sign 5: although halacha mitzvot require intent — blessing its uncertainty to leniency and stringency do not carry as siman 297 — see there. And if he said days and not weeks — apparently doubt to bless lest mitzvot require intent and lest he did not fulfill in days alone — Magen Avraham 4; Peri Chadash here; requires study`,
  "peri-megadim:9:_": `He opened — Taz Magen Avraham 12. It is difficult: if so the question returns to its place — why is uncertainty relevant here since counting was proper only blessing is uncertain — obviously uncertainty of blessings to leniency; even per those who hold counting nowadays d'oraisa; Taz sign 6 one who counted without blessing fulfilled; and granted if uncertainty in counting itself fine per d'oraisa he returns counts and blesses also like Shema siman 67 — see there Taz 1 Magen Avraham — see there; Avodat Yom Tov and Eshel Avraham; requires study`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:1:_": `Seif 1: One begins to count. If counting in writing — is it counting? See Teshuvot Magen Shmuel siman 20. If he counted by way of twenty minus one he fulfilled — responsum Pri HaEretz to Maharam Mizrachi siman 10. That which one does not bless shehecheyanu on counting the omer — see responsum Radbaz siman 54`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:2:_": `Magen Avraham s.k. 2: And if he wishes, one who hears the blessing. It is good that one bless and fulfill others to fulfill "in the multitude of the people is the glory of the king" — see above siman 288 seif 18. See responsum Mahari Weil siman 280`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:3:_": `Seif 7: If he forgot and did not bless all night — if a deceased fell to him at night and the burial ground is distant such that he is an onen all night and the next day he counts the entire day without blessing, and on the days after he counts with blessing — responsum Noda B'Yehuda siman 27`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:10:_": `49. Ba'er Heitev; and see in Chayei Adam who wrote in the name of Maharil that when he reaches day 49 he says they are seven weeks and the next day is erev Shavuot — this is astonishing, for it implies he says so at the time of counting, and this is not so; this wording "and the next day" etc. is the language of Lekutei Maharil who wrote this as the manner of customs that day 49 is erev Shavuot, but not that one says so at the time of counting; and in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 3 they challenged him and he strained to prove from what is written "and the next day" etc., and it is not so; and likewise the practice of the people is that they do not say this at all`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:3:_": `On — Ba'er Heitev; and see in She'elat Ya'avetz siman 139 who disagrees that even if he does not understand in the holy tongue he fulfilled — see there`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:8:_": `In the omer. Ba'er Heitev; and see in responsum Beit Yaakov that his father-in-law the gaon would say "to the omer" and he upheld the nusach "to the omer" and brought proof from Chayei Adam siman 253 — see there; but in truth there is no proof from there to here; and we find in the language of Scripture "in the second year of their going out" and similar; it is better "to the omer" with the lamed, and so is practiced in our country per the Ari; and see in Even HaEzer siman 126 regarding writing a get for the month — see there`,
};
